From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 02:39:22 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Embedding: let a host that owns guest memory drive FEXCore

An embedding host (EnableEmbedding) keeps guest memory behind its own
software TLB and runs every guest process in one host process. The
backend translates each guest access inline with a cold miss path into
a shared dispatcher stub, block entries spend an instruction budget,
SYSCALL/INT 0x80 and guest exceptions leave the dispatch, the decoder
reads guest code through host pointers, CPUID is never folded, and
x87 state and the register cache are precise at every instruction.
An exit reports only the instructions that completed, a store into
the code of the unit running it ends that unit first, and DIV and
IDIV raise #DE before they write. Gathers and masked moves translate
each element, a locked operand that crosses a 16-byte granule runs
on a host copy under the host's lock and is written back with
EMBEDDER_ACCESS_COMMIT, and a repeated CMPS leaves each element's
flags. A compile checks every write against its buffer's end and
restarts with a larger buffer, so growth never depends on a guard
page.
The host has no other translator to hand an instruction to: one the
dispatcher refuses, and FEXCore's reserved escapes 0F 3E and 0F 3F,
raise #UD. A branch or return to guest address 0 finds no lookup
entry, as for any other address without code. A 32-bit push stores
before ESP changes. In 64-bit mode MOV to
FS or GS changes the selector and keeps the base the host set.
Darwin: executable memory, its release and its write window come only
from hooks the host must install, so FEXCore maps no executable memory
itself; x18 stays reserved, and VirtualDontNeed zeroes.
